Subject: [PATCH] drivers: can: stm32_bxcan: fix CAN mailbox overwrite

Backport of upstream Zephyr 4b276c496201c27abfde4cd8cff2b3d56c8a08bf
(Mikhail Siomin, 2024-04-19), which landed after our pinned v3.6.0.

Baseline: zephyr v3.6.0 (468eb56cf242eedba62006ee758700ee6148763f)
Applies to: drivers/can/can_stm32_bxcan.c (can_stm32_send)

WHY WE NEED IT, measured on DS20001 2026-08-20. can_stm32_send() reads the
transmit status register BEFORE taking inst_mutex and then selects a TX
mailbox from that stale value inside the critical section. Two threads
sending concurrently can therefore both read "mailbox 0 free" and both
select it; the second one overwrites mb0->callback_arg, so the TX-complete
interrupt signals only the second sender's completion object.

That matters here because z_impl_can_send() implements the callback == NULL
form as:

    err = api->send(dev, frame, timeout, can_tx_default_cb, &ctx);
    if (err != 0) return err;
    k_sem_take(&ctx.done, K_FOREVER);

The caller's timeout bounds only "wait for a free mailbox" -- waiting for
the send to COMPLETE is an unconditional K_FOREVER. So the sender whose
callback_arg was overwritten never wakes up.

Observed consequence: zcan_main (the single thread that drives every CAN
poll() on this board) sat in state `pending` with `timeout: 0` forever.
SCB stopped publishing 0x206/0x207/0x209/0x20A/0x20C/0x212/0x204 entirely,
and the ROS->SCB board-control frame 0x20F -- emergency stop, power off,
wheel power, heartbeat, lockdown -- was no longer consumed, so its 16-deep
msgq overflowed continuously at the 31 Hz the host was sending. The CAN
controller itself was healthy throughout: ERROR-ACTIVE, zero TX/RX error
counters. It is a driver-level callback mismatch, not a bus fault.

The second concurrent sender was introduced by the cliff ToF health
heartbeat, which runs on the system work queue and sends 0x217 on the same
can2 controller every 200 ms. Before that, can2 had exactly one synchronous
sender (zcan_main serialises its eleven poll() calls), which is why this
latent driver defect had never been reachable in this repository.

Neither test layer could have caught it: the host suites replace the CAN
sink with a function pointer (no driver, no concurrency), and the
TOF_CLIFF_BUDGET probe runs from SYS_INIT before zcan_main's thread exists.

NOT A COMPLETE FIX for shared synchronous sending. With all three mailboxes
busy, both senders still wait on data->tx_int_sem, which has a limit of 1,
so one of them returns -EAGAIN and drops a frame. That path is BOUNDED and
therefore acceptable as an interim state; a single TX broker (one thread
owning can2, others posting to a msgq) is the real fix and is deliberately
deferred so that it does not change send-completion semantics during the
cliff E2E bring-up.

diff --git a/drivers/can/can_stm32_bxcan.c b/drivers/can/can_stm32_bxcan.c
index 77eba66e7d8..c21099c04b3 100644
--- a/drivers/can/can_stm32_bxcan.c
+++ b/drivers/can/can_stm32_bxcan.c
@@ -761,7 +761,7 @@ static int can_stm32_send(const struct device *dev, const struct can_frame *fram
 	const struct can_stm32_config *cfg = dev->config;
 	struct can_stm32_data *data = dev->data;
 	CAN_TypeDef *can = cfg->can;
-	uint32_t transmit_status_register = can->TSR;
+	uint32_t transmit_status_register = 0;
 	CAN_TxMailBox_TypeDef *mailbox = NULL;
 	struct can_stm32_mailbox *mb = NULL;
 
@@ -793,6 +793,7 @@ static int can_stm32_send(const struct device *dev, const struct can_frame *fram
 	}
 
 	k_mutex_lock(&data->inst_mutex, K_FOREVER);
+	transmit_status_register = can->TSR;
 	while (!(transmit_status_register & CAN_TSR_TME)) {
 		k_mutex_unlock(&data->inst_mutex);
 		LOG_DBG("Transmit buffer full");
